{"word": "surround", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "surround", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/səˈɹaʊnd/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "surrounds", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "surrounding", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "surrounded", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "surrounded", "tags": ["past"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(transitive) To encircle something or simultaneously extend in all directions.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "The hovel stood in the centre of what had once been a vegetable garden, but was now a patch of rank weeds. Surrounding this, almost like a zareba, was an irregular ring of gorse and brambles, an unclaimed vestige of the original common.", "reference": "1944, Miles Burton, chapter 5, in The Three Corpse Trick:"}, {"text": "It took a long time for the place to warm up and to counteract the cold and enable their fingers to cope with the delicate task of moulding, the men would often surround themselves with blocks of iron heated in the furnace.", "reference": "1944 November and December, A Former Pupil, “Some Memories of Crewe Works—II”, in Railway Magazine, page 342:"}, {"text": "Sepia Delft tiles surrounded the fireplace, their crudely drawn Biblical scenes in faded cyclamen blending with the pinkish pine, while above them, instead of a mantelshelf, there was an archway high enough to form a balcony with slender balusters and a tapestry-hung wall behind.", "reference": "1963, Margery Allingham, “Miss Thyrza’s Chair”, in The China Governess: A Mystery, London: Chatto & Windus, →OCLC, page 41:"}, {"text": "and this way they get rid of those grand and stubborn opinions that surround them.", "reference": "2005, Plato, translated by Lesley Brown, Sophist, page 230c:"}, {"text": "The superficial fascia surrounds the body and includes subcu